  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Explore the XAA Protocol</p>
      <p class="tagline">Enabling secure cross-application access through enterprise identity providers</p>
    </section>

    <section class="intro">
      <h2>What is the XAA Protocol?</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant</strong> (ID-JAG), also known as the <strong>XAA (Cross App Access)</strong> protocol, 
        is an emerging IETF standard that enables applications to obtain access tokens for third-party APIs by coordinating 
        through a common enterprise identity provider (IdP).
      </p>
      <p>
        This specification combines <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8693" target="_blank">OAuth 2.0 Token Exchange (RFC 8693)</a> 
        and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7523" target="_blank">JWT Profile for OAuth 2.0 Authorization Grants (RFC 7523)</a> 
        to provide a seamless, user-friendly way for enterprise applications to access each other's APIs without requiring 
        additional interactive OAuth flows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="problem">
      <h2>The Problem It Solves</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🔐 Traditional OAuth Flows</h3>
          <p>
            When one application needs to access a user's data at another application, it typically starts an interactive 
            OAuth flow. This requires user interaction and creates a direct app-to-app connection that is not visible to 
            the enterprise IdP.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🏢 Enterprise Control</h3>
          <p>
            Enterprises want centralized control over which applications can access each other's APIs on behalf of users. 
            The XAA protocol enables the same IdP that manages single sign-on to also broker API access between applications.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>👤 User Experience</h3>
          <p>
            Multiple OAuth consent screens create friction. XAA enables seamless cross-application access without 
            additional user interaction, while maintaining proper authorization through the enterprise IdP.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="how-it-works">
      <h2>How It Works</h2>
      <div class="flow-diagram">
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-number">1</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>User SSO</h4>
            <p>User authenticates with the IdP. The client obtains an identity assertion (ID Token or SAML assertion).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-number">2</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Token Exchange</h4>
            <p>Client uses the identity assertion to request an Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant (ID-JAG) from the IdP for the target resource.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-number">3</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>Exchange ID-JAG</h4>
            <p>Client exchanges the ID-JAG for an access token at the Resource Authorization Server's token endpoint.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-number">4</div>
          <div class="step-content">
            <h4>API Access</h4>
            <p>Client makes API requests to the Resource Server using the obtained access token.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="key-concepts">
      <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
      <div class="concepts-grid">
        <div class="concept">
          <h4>Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant (ID-JAG)</h4>
          <p>
            A JWT issued by the IdP that grants a client delegated access to a resource in another trust domain 
            on behalf of a user. It uses the media type <code>oauth-id-jag+jwt</code>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="concept">
          <h4>Token Exchange</h4>
          <p>
            The client uses RFC 8693 Token Exchange to request an ID-JAG from the IdP, providing the user's 
            identity assertion, the target audience, and requested scopes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="concept">
          <h4>JWT Bearer Grant</h4>
          <p>
            The ID-JAG is presented to the Resource Authorization Server using the RFC 7523 JWT Bearer 
            assertion grant type to obtain an access token.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="concept">
          <h4>Trust Relationships</h4>
          <p>
            Both the Client and Resource Authorization Server must have established trust relationships 
            with the common IdP for SSO and ID-JAG issuance.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="use-cases">
      <h2>Use Cases</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🏗️ Enterprise SaaS Integration</h3>
          <p>
            Enable seamless API access between enterprise SaaS applications (e.g., a wiki app accessing 
            a chat app's API) without requiring users to complete multiple OAuth consent flows.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>📧 Email & Calendaring</h3>
          <p>
            Email clients can access third-party calendaring APIs using the user's existing identity 
            assertion, streamlining the user experience in native applications.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>🤖 LLM Agents & AI Tools</h3>
          <p>
            AI agents can access enterprise tools and APIs on behalf of users, with all access controlled 
            and auditable through the enterprise IdP.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="id-jag-structure">
      <h2>ID-JAG Structure</h2>
      <p>An Identity Assertion JWT Authorization Grant contains the following claims:</p>
      <div class="code-block">
        <pre><code>{
  "typ": "oauth-id-jag+jwt"
}
.
{
  "jti": "9e43f81b64a33f20116179",
  "iss": "https://acme.idp.example",
  "sub": "U019488227",
  "aud": "https://acme.chat.example/",
  "client_id": "f53f191f9311af35",
  "exp": 1311281970,
  "iat": 1311280970,
  "resource": "https://acme.chat.example/api",
  "scope": "chat.read chat.history",
  "auth_time": 1311280970,
  "amr": ["mfa", "phrh", "hwk", "user"]
}
.
signature</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="claims-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Claim</th>
              <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><code>iss</code></td>
              <td>The issuer identifier of the IdP Authorization Server</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>sub</code></td>
              <td>The subject identifier (user ID) at the Resource Authorization Server</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>aud</code></td>
              <td>The issuer identifier of the Resource Authorization Server</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>client_id</code></td>
              <td>The identifier of the client acting on behalf of the user</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>resource</code></td>
              <td>The Resource Identifier of the Resource Server (optional)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><code>scope</code></td>
              <td>Space-separated list of requested scopes (optional)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

